This setup makes two products, CPU and EV_battery, using three purchased inputs: plastic_pellet, solder_paste, and epoxy_compound. The network links GlassCore Ltd., ElectroParts, and PolymerSource with three internal sites: Pilot Line, Processing Unit, and Storage Facility. At Pilot Line, the bonding step turns solder_paste, plastic_pellet, and epoxy_compound into CPU, and each run takes exactly 14.0 days. The bill of materials is fairly material-heavy: one CPU uses 1 unit of solder_paste, 4 units of plastic_pellet, and 4 units of epoxy_compound. Processing Unit handles EV_battery production through an inspection step that consumes 3 units of epoxy_compound per EV_battery and runs in exactly 22.0 days. The shared shop resource is the Assembly Line, with capacity for 4 units, an exponentially distributed service time averaging 7.044 days, and an operating cost of $14.627 per day. For raw materials, plastic_pellet is controlled with a min-max style policy: when stock falls below 3398.37 units, it is replenished up to 7932.56 units. Its shortage penalty is $14.752 per unit, and inventory is reviewed every 8 days. GlassCore Ltd. supplies plastic_pellet at $124.049 per unit, with lead time averaging 23.0 days, payment due 11.0 days after delivery, and shipment from GlassCore Ltd. to Pilot Line takes 7.0 to 7.0 days. Solder_paste is sourced as needed based on demand, reviewed every 7 days, and carries a holding cost of $1.109 per unit. ElectroParts provides it at $341.411 per unit, on a fixed 22.0-day lead time, with 83.0-day payment terms and a delivery limit of 529042 units per order. Epoxy_compound is also ordered on demand. It is reviewed every 27 days, with a shortage cost of $17.889 per unit. PolymerSource sells it for $111.653 per unit, with supply lead time varying uniformly between 9.0 days and 28.0 days, and payment is made 29.0 days after receipt. On the finished-goods side, CPU starts with 2946858 units in stock, has a holding cost of $0.214 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 day. EV_battery opens with 790516 units on hand. Output from both Pilot Line and Processing Unit is sent to Storage Facility for warehousing. Customer demand comes from two major accounts. Dell Technologies buys CPU on a regular 17.0-day cycle, with each order fixed at 3556913.0 units. Delivery to Dell Technologies takes between 1.0 day and 6.0 days, the selling price is $3763.405 per unit, customer payments arrive in 41.0 to 84.0 days, and any shortfall is backordered rather than lost. Lenovo Group purchases EV_battery every 22.0 days, with demand centered around 1345747.0 units and varying by about 307335.0 units. Shipments to Lenovo Group take between 7.0 days and 12.0 days, the sales price is $2440.283 per unit, payment comes in 16.0 to 30.0 days, and if stock is unavailable, the sale is lost. The model is run in days over a 730-day horizon with 5 replications.

