This network centers on making smartphones and laptops from a shared pool of materials, with suppliers SiliconWorks, PrecisionParts, ChipCo, CarbonSupply Co., BatteryTech, MetalFab Inc., and ElectroParts feeding Assembly Hub, Manufacturing Site, and Fulfillment Hub over a 500-day planning horizon with 10 replications. The basic inputs are copper_wire, carbon_fiber, microprocessor, and solder_paste. A cooling_unit is built from 5 units of microprocessor, 3 units of copper_wire, 5 units of carbon_fiber, and 3 units of solder_paste, while a power_module uses 2 units of carbon_fiber and a sensor_array uses 5 units of solder_paste. On the finished goods side, each smartphone requires 1 unit of power_module, 2 units of copper_wire, 4 units of carbon_fiber, and 3 units of solder_paste, and each laptop uses 3 units of microprocessor, 1 unit of cooling_unit, 2 units of sensor_array, 1 unit of carbon_fiber, and 1 unit of solder_paste. Copper_wire is sourced on an as-needed basis, with a shortage penalty of $31.993 per unit and inventory reviewed every 21 days. Carbon_fiber is also demand-driven, carrying a holding cost of $8.268 per unit, a shortage cost of $43.817 per unit, and a 10-day review cycle. Microprocessor replenishment is likewise triggered by demand, with a shortage cost of $44.457 per unit and review every 19 days. Solder_paste works differently: it arrives in batches averaging 47807.0 units, varying by about 351.0 units, and those deliveries are scheduled somewhere between 3.0 days and 28.0 days apart. Supplier terms vary quite a bit. SiliconWorks provides copper_wire in exactly 5.0 days at $250.896 per unit, with payment due between 25.0 days and 41.0 days after receipt, and it can supply up to 463696 units per order; shipments from SiliconWorks to Assembly Hub take between 4.0 days and 8.0 days. BatteryTech is the second copper_wire source, with lead times ranging from 12.0 days to 25.0 days, a purchase cost of $328.334 per unit, and net 47.0-day payment. PrecisionParts supplies carbon_fiber with an exponential lead time averaging 8.0 days, charges $414.8 per unit, and gives 70.0-day payment terms. MetalFab Inc. is another carbon_fiber supplier, typically around 23.0 days lead time, at $433.665 per unit, payable 27.0 days after delivery, and limited to 90164 units per delivery; its transfer to Assembly Hub takes between 8.0 days and 9.0 days. For microprocessors, ChipCo delivers somewhere between 12.0 days and 27.0 days, charges $363.425 per unit, and is paid between 9.0 days and 85.0 days after delivery, with a 6.0-day transfer into Assembly Hub. ElectroParts offers a tighter 16.0-day to 17.0-day lead time for microprocessor supply at $359.28 per unit and 88.0-day payment terms. CarbonSupply Co. handles solder_paste, with an exponential lead time averaging 26.0 days, a cost of $399.394 per unit, and payment due between 8.0 days and 61.0 days. Inside the network, Assembly Hub manages solder_paste, carbon_fiber, smartphone, power_module, and copper_wire inventory. Its inspection step converts power_module, copper_wire, carbon_fiber, and solder_paste into smartphone output in a fixed 28.0-day cycle and depends on the Quality Scanner. That scanner has capacity for 2 units at a time, runs with a constant 2.343-day service time, and costs $7.571 per day to operate. Manufacturing Site manages microprocessor, laptop, sensor_array, cooling_unit, solder_paste, and carbon_fiber, and its cutting operation turns microprocessor, cooling_unit, sensor_array, carbon_fiber, and solder_paste into laptops in anywhere from 6.0 days to 29.0 days. Fulfillment Hub acts as the warehouse receiving smartphone output from Assembly Hub and laptop output from Manufacturing Site. Starting stock is extremely large for the in-process and finished items: 3563552 units of cooling_unit, 4337440 units of power_module, 1582423 units of sensor_array, 1792340 units of smartphone, and 1009381 units of laptop. Cooling_unit inventory is reviewed every 1 day with a shortage cost of $46.503 per unit. Power_module is also reviewed every 1 day and has a shortage cost of $2.437 per unit. Sensor_array carries 1582423 units initially, with a holding cost of $4.104 per unit, a shortage penalty of $23.202 per unit, and 1-day reviews. Smartphone inventory starts at 1792340 units, costs $2.852 per unit to hold, has a shortage cost of $7.93 per unit, and is checked every 1 day. Laptop begins with 1009381 units, with holding at $2.405 per unit, shortage at $17.297 per unit, and the same 1-day review cadence. Customer demand is sizable. Infineon Technologies buys smartphone orders every 26.0 days, always for 3962639.0 units, and expects delivery in 3.0 days to 7.0 days. If stock is short, the order is backordered rather than lost. Smartphones sell to this customer at $1404.377 per unit, and cash comes in 66.0 days after delivery. Schneider Electric purchases laptops with arrivals averaging 43.0 days, each order fixed at 4149566.0 units, and a customer lead time of exactly 28.0 days. Here the business follows a lost sales approach, so if product is unavailable the sale is lost. The laptop selling price is $3334.991 per unit, and payment comes in 41.0 days, effectively a fixed 41.0-day term. Overall, the structure is straightforward: multiple upstream suppliers feed Assembly Hub, smartphones move from Assembly Hub to Fulfillment Hub, laptops move from Manufacturing Site to Fulfillment Hub, and the whole setup runs day by day across 500 days.

