ep converts carbon_fiber, circuit_board, and solder_paste into router output in exactly 2.0 weeks. A separate polishing step turns solder_paste into CPU output in a 15.0 to 20.0 week cycle. Inventory starts high across the downstream stages: 3168331 units of circuit_board, 2331093 units of router, and 3590609 units of CPU. Carrying circuit_board costs $9.471 per unit, shortages cost $41.416 per unit, and review happens every 1 week. Router inventory carries at $6.245 per unit, with shortage penalties of $32.452 per unit and a 1 week review cycle. CPU stock has a holding cost of $9.022 per unit, a shortage cost of $48.449 per unit, and is also checked every 1 week. Customer demand comes from two named accounts. HP Enterprise buys router, placing orders every 23.0 weeks for 4270713.0 units each time, expects delivery in 7.0 weeks, and any shortfall is backordered rather than lost. Those routers sell at $4649.787 per unit, and customer payment comes in between 14.0 days and 51.0 days. Broadcom Limited buys CPU, with orders arriving every 12.0 weeks and volumes ranging from 629480.0 units to 4667721.0 units; delivery commitment runs between 14.0 weeks and 20.0 weeks, and if stock is unavailable, the sale is lost. CPU sells at $1758.701 per unit, with customer payment received between 11.0 days and 19.0 days. Material flows from the three suppliers into the Integration Center, and both router and CPU then move on to the Distribution Center. The model runs for 365 weeks overall, with the first 30 weeks treated as warm-up, and it uses 5 replications.

