g $326.524 per unit, offering net 50.0 days, and limiting each order to 878215 units; its lane to the plant takes 9.0 weeks. Inside the plant, starting inventories are substantial: 3013859 units of sensor_array, 763394 units of cooling_unit, 4092911 units of circuit_board, 4897102 units of GPU, 2182909 units of drone, and 1379560 units of laptop. Sensor_array is reviewed every 1 week and carries a shortage cost of $10.192 per unit. Cooling_unit is also checked every 1 week, with a holding cost of $3.649 per unit. Circuit_board has 1-week reviews as well, with $2.601 per unit in holding cost and $2.152 per unit in shortage cost. Finished GPU stock is reviewed every 1 week and has a shortage penalty of $0.731 per unit, drone inventory is reviewed every 1 week with a holding cost of $0.634 per unit, and laptop inventory is reviewed every 1 week with $4.202 per unit holding cost and $20.509 per unit shortage cost. Production is centered at the Manufacturing Site. The coating step turns solder_paste, circuit_board, resin, and cooling_unit into GPU, takes exactly 20.0 weeks per cycle, and uses the Robot Arm. That Robot Arm has capacity for 3 units, works with an exponential service time averaging 8.455 weeks, batches jobs in lots of 8 units, waits up to 1.383 weeks to form a batch, and costs $9.807 per week to operate. The inspection step converts sensor_array, cooling_unit, and solder_paste into drone in 5.0 weeks flat, while the bonding step makes laptop from resin and ceramic_substrate in a 2.0 to 4.0 week range. The Manufacturing Site manages circuit_board, GPU, cooling_unit, solder_paste, and resin in one flow, drone, solder_paste, sensor_array, and cooling_unit in another, and laptop, resin, and ceramic_substrate in the third. Customer demand is split across three accounts. Broadcom Limited buys GPU, placing orders every 14.0 to 46.0 weeks, with demand centered around 4574450.0 units and varying by about 1003147.0 units; customer lead time is 4.0 weeks, shortages are backordered, the selling price is $4890.373 per unit, and payment arrives 33.0 days after delivery. Schneider Electric orders drone with arrivals averaging 20.0 weeks, order sizes between 2476389.0 units and 3800872.0 units, a delivery promise of 7.0 weeks, and a partial-backorder setup where available units ship immediately and the remainder waits; the price is $1431.051 per unit and payment comes in 54.0 days. Ross Associates purchases laptop, ordering between 25.0 weeks and 29.0 weeks apart, always for 712780.0 units, with a 15.0-week lead time; if stock is unavailable, the sale is lost, laptops sell at $771.414 per unit, and customer payment lands between 16.0 days and 44.0 days. Structurally, the node design links the 3 suppliers—Memory Star, SiliconWorks, and MegaSupply—to the 2 facilities, Manufacturing Site and Storage Facility, and finished GPUs, drones, and laptops all flow from Manufacturing Site into Storage Facility. The model runs over a 500-week horizon with 1 replication, and results are collected after a 50-week warm-up period.

