This setup makes camera units using three purchased inputs at Pilot Line, with Distribution Center handling the finished camera flow downstream. Each camera requires 1 unit of microprocessor, 1 unit of steel_rod, and 3 units of gold_wire. For microprocessor stock, the business follows a min-max style rule: when inventory drops below 1498.57 units, it replenishes up to 2196.84 units. Those microprocessors come from GlassCore Ltd. with a supplier lead time ranging from 11.0 hours to 25.0 hours, a purchase cost of $60.847 per unit, and payment due 38.0 days after delivery. After shipment leaves GlassCore Ltd., transfer to Pilot Line takes between 6.0 hours and 11.0 hours. Microprocessor inventory is reviewed every 15 hours and carries a holding cost of $6.088 per unit. Steel_rod is sourced from FastChip Corp. under another reorder policy, with replenishment triggered below 536.15 units and raised to 852.4 units. FastChip Corp. delivers in exactly 20.0 hours, charges $260.35 per unit, offers payment terms anywhere from 27.0 days to 43.0 days, and can supply up to 399419 units per order. Gold_wire is handled differently: it is supplied in regular lots ranging from 17072.0 units to 28860.0 units, with deliveries arriving every 8.0 hours. BatteryTech provides that material with an exponentially distributed lead time averaging 11.0 hours, at $32.879 per unit, and payment is made 39.0 days after receipt. Gold_wire inventory is reviewed every 12 hours, and any shortage costs $42.632 per unit. Manufacturing happens at Pilot Line through an operation called polishing, which consumes microprocessor, steel_rod, and gold_wire and turns them into camera units in a fixed 22.0-hour cycle. On the finished goods side, camera inventory starts at 2929335 units, is reviewed every 1 hour, costs $2.447 per unit to hold, and incurs a shortage cost of $11.161 per unit. Demand comes from TechCorp, with customer orders arriving on an exponential pattern averaging 29.0 hours, each request fixed at 3788823.0 units. Customer deliveries take exactly 21.0 hours, and the company uses a partial-fill lost-sales approach: available camera units are shipped, and the remainder of any shortfall is lost. Cameras sell at $4870.619 per unit, and TechCorp pays between 38.0 days and 53.0 days later. The network links GlassCore Ltd., FastChip Corp., and BatteryTech into Pilot Line, and then sends finished camera units from Pilot Line to Distribution Center. The model runs in hours over a 730-hour horizon with 10 replications.

