, and runs in batches of 53 units with a maximum wait of 10.184 days before processing, at an operating cost of $12.502 per day. Camera assembly is also done at the Integration Center, using carbon_fiber only, and its cycle time varies uniformly between 16.0 days and 24.0 days. Once built, both FPGA and camera are sent from the Integration Center to the Storage Facility. Customer demand is split across two accounts. Bosch Automotive buys FPGA, placing orders every 23.0 to 49.0 days, with demand centered around 1003693.0 units and varying by about 180108.0 units. Orders are delivered in exactly 9.0 days, and if stock is short, available units are shipped immediately while the remainder is backordered. The FPGA selling price is $1023.411 per unit, and Bosch Automotive pays in 18.0 to 29.0 days. Ross Associates purchases camera on a very regular cadence, ordering every 33.0 days with constant demand of 3377235.0 units. Camera shipments reach the customer in 1.0 day, and if inventory is unavailable, the sale is lost. That product sells at $3863.548 per unit, with customer payment arriving between 19.0 days and 38.0 days. The modeled network explicitly links MetalFab Inc., MegaSupply, and ChipCo to the Integration Center, and then routes FPGA and camera onward to the Storage Facility. The scenario runs over a 500-day horizon, with results collected after a 30-day warm-up period and evaluated across 5 replications.

